@Tutorial(time: 15) {
    @Intro(title: "Navigate to detail") {
        Give It Structure put the detail beside the list with ``SplitView``.
        This chapter builds the other shape: ``NavigationStack`` pushes the
        detail over the list, and Esc pops back. Same store, same rows — a
        different answer to where the detail goes, and a feel for when each
        answer wins.

        Continue from the third tutorial's finished code, or
        [download the finished project](https://dardevelin.github.io/terminal-ui/downloads/build-a-task-list.zip)
        as the base to edit.

        @Image(source: "demo-ch4.gif", alt: "Pushing a task detail, toggling it, popping back.")
    }

    @Section(title: "Push instead of split") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A ``NavigationStack`` shows one screen at a time: its root, until
            a value is pushed — then the screen that
            `navigationDestination(for:)` builds from that value.
            ``NavigationLink`` rows do the pushing, and the path is a plain
            array on the store: links append to it, Esc pops it, and the
            stack renders whatever the top element resolves to.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Swap the Tasks tab's ``SplitView`` for a ``NavigationStack``:
                the `SplitState` and the side-by-side detail pane go, a
                `path: [Int]` lands on the store, and each row becomes a
                ``NavigationLink`` carrying its task's id.
                `navigationDestination(for: Int.self)` turns a pushed id
                into a `TaskDetail` screen.

                The arrow and Enter cases leave `onEvent` too: links are
                focusable, so the focus engine moves between rows and Enter
                activates the focused one. Esc stays — it still quits at the
                root, but while a screen is pushed the runtime hands Esc to
                the stack instead, which pops one level.

                @Code(name: "main.swift", file: "tasks4-01-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "preview-tasks4-01-01.png", alt: "A pushed Detail screen for one task. The list is gone; only the tab bar remains around it.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Act on the pushed screen") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A detail screen that can only be looked at sends the user back
            to the list for every change. Let it edit instead: the screen
            carries nothing but the task's id and reads and writes the same
            store the list does, so popping needs no save step — the list
            is already right when it reappears.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Put a ``Toggle`` on the detail screen, bound to the store
                through the pushed id. Open a task, press Space, press Esc:
                the screen pops and the row's checkbox already agrees with
                the toggle.

                Watch where focus lands after the pop — on the exact link
                that pushed. A pushed screen is presented the way a modal
                is: the covered list is unreachable while it is up, and the
                same machinery restores the previous focus on the way out,
                so a long list never loses the user's place.

                @Code(name: "main.swift", file: "tasks4-02-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "preview-tasks4-02-01.png", alt: "The pushed detail with a focused, checked Done toggle.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Show the trail") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The title row is the one piece of chrome a pushed screen keeps,
            so make it earn its cells: a breadcrumb-style title says where
            the user is and how they got there, and a hint line documents
            the two keys the screen answers to.

            Up next: <doc:ShowTheNumbers> adds a Stats tab — tables,
            sparklines, and the judgment of which display form fits.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Set the detail's `navigationTitle` to a breadcrumb — `Tasks
                ▸` followed by the task's title — and add a `Space toggle
                Esc back` hint line under the fields.

                You now hold both shapes, built on one store. The split
                shows list and detail at once — reach for it when the user
                scans one while referring to the other and the context must
                persist. The stack shows one thing at a time — reach for it
                when a shallow drill-down deserves the whole screen and a
                guaranteed way back. The store never changed between
                chapters, which is the point: navigation shape is a
                view-layer decision.

                @Code(name: "main.swift", file: "tasks4-03-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "preview-tasks4-03-01.png", alt: "The detail screen titled with a breadcrumb: Tasks, an arrow, then the task's name.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            A mail client lets the user scan the message list while reading one message. Which shape fits?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                A ``SplitView`` — list and detail stay on screen together.

                @Justification(reaction: "Right.") {
                    Scanning-while-reading needs both at once and a context
                    that persists; that is the split's whole case.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                A ``NavigationStack`` — reading pushes the message over the list.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    A push replaces the screen, so the list the user wants
                    to keep scanning disappears with every open.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                A ``TabView`` — one tab for the list, one for the message.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    Tabs are for peer screens with no hierarchy; a message
                    is subordinate to the list, not a sibling of it.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            While the detail screen is up, where does the task's `done` flag live?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                On the shared store — the pushed value is only the task's
                id, and the screen reads and writes through it.

                @Justification(reaction: "Right.") {
                    That is why popping needs no save step and why the
                    list's checkbox already agrees when it reappears.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                In the pushed screen's own state, copied back to the store
                on pop.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    Nothing is copied in either direction — the ``Toggle``'s
                    binding mutates the store directly, while the screen is
                    still up.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                In the navigation path, alongside the pushed id.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    The path holds only the ids that select screens; it
                    carries no task data at all.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            After Esc pops the detail, why is the link that pushed it focused again?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                A pushed screen is presented like a modal — the focus
                engine fences the covered list off and restores the
                previously focused node on dismissal.

                @Justification(reaction: "Right.") {
                    Push reuses the modal containment machinery, and focus
                    restoration on pop is part of that contract.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Focus always returns to the first focusable view after any
                screen change.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    That would lose the user's place in a long list — pop
                    restores the *specific* link that pushed, not the first.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                The path binding records which link had focus and reapplies
                it.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    The path holds pushed values only; focus bookkeeping
                    belongs to the focus engine, not to your state.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
